INSERT INTO part (id, message_id, session_id, time_created, time_updated, data) VALUES ('prt_0308', 'msg_0078', 'opencode-real-sanitized', 1786710282095, 1786710282095, '{"text":"Codex reasoning uses `summary` array (which is empty here) and `content` (which is null in this case) — and `encrypted_content` for the actual reasoning (encrypted, unavailable). In newer Codex versions, reasoning `summary` may contain `[{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"...\"}]`. So my reader handles the case where summary is non-empty. When the content is encrypted, there''s nothing extractable — that''s a Codex privacy feature. My handling is correct: skip when empty. Fine. The events-hash difference between source and roundtrip is due to JSON key ordering (serde_json Map sorts keys → `max_output_tokens` moves before `workdir`). Semantically identical. Acceptable. Everything works. Let me update todos and clean up. Actually, let me reconsider the roundtrip fidelity issue about `serde_json::Value` Map key sorting. When re-reading the imported OpenCode session, arguments serializes sorted. This doesn''t affect correctness for the new agent (it reads JSON). Fine. One more thing: I should double check the OpenCode reader `resolve_session_file` handling in read_trace for opencode with session containing \".db\". That''s a bit hacky. Let me simplify: in `Command::Export` for opencode, I already pass the session id and db. `read_trace` for OpenCode does: if session contains \".db\" return opencode::read(db, session) — this is wrong because it passes the full \"path(.db)\" as session_id... wait no: `opencode::read(db, session)` where session contains \".db\" — that would query session_id = \"something.db\". That''s wrong. Let me look at the code again: ```rust AgentKind::OpenCode => { let sid = if session.contains(\".db\") { // called with an opencode db path directly return opencode::read(db, session); } else { session.to_string() }; opencode::read(db, &sid) } ``` This is confusing and buggy. If user passes a session id that happens to contain \".db\" (unlikely but possible in a uuid? no). The intent was to allow `export opencode <dbpath>` maybe. This is over-engineered and wrong. Let me simp\n[TRUNCATED]","time":{"end":1786710287477,"start":1786710282095},"type":"reasoning"}');
INSERT INTO part (id, message_id, session_id, time_created, time_updated, data) VALUES ('prt_0309', 'msg_0078', 'opencode-real-sanitized', 1786710287479, 1786710287479, '{"text":"Codex reasoning uses `encrypted_content` (unavailable by design) — my reader correctly skips empty summaries. Let me clean up a confusing special-case in `read_trace` for opencode:","time":{"end":1786710288258,"start":1786710287479},"type":"text"}');
